window.docContents[211].resolve({"/#checking-polymorphic-types":{"contents":"Checking the type of a function that takes implicit arguments or uses type classes requires the use of some additional syntax.\nSimply writing\n\n\n\nyields a type with metavariables:\n\n\n\nThis is because Lean does its best to discover implicit arguments, and the presence of metavariables indicates that it did not yet discover enough type information to do so.\nTo understand the signature of a function, this feature can be suppressed with an at-sign () before the function's name:\n\n\n\n\n\nThere is a u_1 after Type, which uses a feature of Lean that has not yet been introduced.\nFor now, ignore these parameters to Type.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Overloading and Type Classes\u0009Type Classes and Polymorphism","header":"3.2.1. Checking Polymorphic Functions' Types","id":"/#checking-polymorphic-types"},"/#splitting-shortens":{"contents":"It will also be necessary to prove that (splitList xs).snd.length < xs.length.\nBecause  alternates between adding entries to the two lists, it is easiest to prove both statements at once, so the structure of the proof can follow the algorithm used to implement .\nIn other words, it is easiest to prove that .\n\nUnfortunately, the statement is false.\nIn particular,  is . Both output lists have length , which is not less than , the length of the input list.\nSimilarly,  evaluates to , and  is not shorter than .\nHowever,  evaluates to , and both of these output lists are shorter than the input list.\n\nIt turns out that the lengths of the output lists are always less than or equal to the length of the input list, but they are only strictly shorter when the input list contains at least two entries.\nIt turns out to be easiest to prove the former statement, then extend it to the latter statement.\nBegin with a theorem statement:\n\n\n\n\n\nBecause  is structurally recursive on the list, the proof should use induction.\nThe structural recursion in  fits a proof by induction perfectly: the base case of the induction matches the base case of the recursion, and the inductive step matches the recursive call.\nThe induction tactic gives two goals:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe goal for the  case can be proved by invoking the simplifier and instructing it to unfold the definition of , because the length of the empty list is less than or equal to the length of the empty list.\nSimilarly, simplifying with  in the  case places  around the lengths in the goal:\n\n\n\n\n\nThis is because the call to  consumes the head of the list , converting it to a , in both the length of the input list and the length of the first output list.\n\nWriting  in Lean is short for .\n is a structure type in the  universe:\n\n\n\nIn other words, a proof of  consists of the  constructor applied to a proof of  in the  field and a proof of  in the  field.\n\nThe cases tactic allows a proof to consider each constructor of a datatype or each potential proof of a proposition in turn.\nIt corresponds to a match expression without recursion.\nUsing cases on a structure results in the structure being broken apart, with an assumption added for each field of the structure, just as a pattern match expression extracts the field of a structure for use in a program.\nBecause structures have only one constructor, using cases on a structure does not result in additional goals.\n\nBecause  is a proof of List.length (splitList xs).fst ≤ List.length xs ∧ List.length (splitList xs).snd ≤ List.length xs, using  results in an assumption that List.length (splitList xs).fst ≤ List.length xs and an assumption that List.length (splitList xs).snd ≤ List.length xs:\n\n\n\n\n\nBecause the goal of the proof is also an , the constructor tactic can be used to apply , resulting in a goal for each argument:\n\n\n\n\n\nThe  goal is identical to the left✝ assumption, so the assumption tactic dispatches it:\n\n\n\n\n\nThe  goal resembles the right✝ assumption, except the goal adds a  only to the length of the input list.\nIt's time to prove that the inequality holds.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Programming, Proving, and Performance\u0009More Inequalities","header":"8.4.2. Splitting a List Makes it Shorter","id":"/#splitting-shortens"},"/#typed-query-exercises":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Programming with Dependent Types\u0009Worked Example: Typed Queries","header":"7.3.6. Exercises","id":"/#typed-query-exercises"}});